1
00:00:18,175 --> 00:00:22,496
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this is a question that's come up recently from quite a few people.

2
00:00:23,876 --> 00:00:28,358
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I've just sort of paraphrased it into one.

3
00:00:29,618 --> 00:00:46,342
[SPEAKER_04]: So, a friend asks, the more I realize that it's impossible for any of my activities to be separate from this body, the nature of the body, the more I sense the confinement that Bhante often mentions, and the more oppressive it feels.

4
00:00:47,952 --> 00:00:57,756
[SPEAKER_04]: As a result, I begin to feel like everything I do is worthless, pointless, self-loathing, sadness, and this overwhelms me.

5
00:00:59,097 --> 00:01:10,501
[SPEAKER_04]: I believe that such thoughts are rooted in ill-will and a wish to just give up, just to give up the practice, become lazy.

6
00:01:11,902 --> 00:01:16,824
[SPEAKER_04]: I would appreciate it if you could give me a few tips on how to go through this kind of state.

7
00:01:17,908 --> 00:01:19,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, by going through it.

8
00:01:20,691 --> 00:01:30,299
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the self-loathing, the worldlessness, the confinement, that is, yeah, all factual.

9
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:45,372
[SPEAKER_02]: But the reason why it is painful, it's because your views, your habits, your expectations, are still kind of referred to from the point of view of centrality.

10
00:01:47,011 --> 00:01:57,158
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the central pleasures, the ambition, the inspiration, spilling out in the world, that is still where your criterias are.

11
00:01:58,382 --> 00:01:58,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Habitually.

12
00:01:59,203 --> 00:02:07,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, now you started reflecting, you saw, no, that doesn't add up, it's actual confinement, it's sense restraint, all of that, and now this is felt unpleasant.

13
00:02:07,709 --> 00:02:17,717
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's felt unpleasantly, not because in and of itself, but it's felt unpleasantly because you're still not fully abandoned the previous kind of views and criterias and notions.

14
00:02:19,018 --> 00:02:20,600
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the self-loathing

15
00:02:21,887 --> 00:02:29,549
[SPEAKER_02]: aspect of of that confinement is due to basically your kind of addiction of sensuality still being there.

16
00:02:30,189 --> 00:02:37,551
[SPEAKER_02]: And the only way to then, for that addiction to to sort of dissipate, is to endure the self-loathing and don't go back to sensuality.

17
00:02:37,571 --> 00:02:44,273
[SPEAKER_02]: They will then re-value it, re-infuse it again with the old value, which will then remove the self-loathing

18
00:02:45,061 --> 00:02:48,802
[SPEAKER_02]: From being immediately felt, and put it back into your framework.

19
00:02:48,982 --> 00:02:53,643
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, sooner or later, when you don't have enough sensuality, you're going to go back, and you're going to start self-loathing again.

20
00:02:54,403 --> 00:02:58,824
[SPEAKER_02]: When the distractions sort of disappear, self-loathing is back.

21
00:02:59,024 --> 00:03:00,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Confinement is back.

22
00:03:01,925 --> 00:03:04,345
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't deal with it by doing this or that.

23
00:03:04,405 --> 00:03:06,726
[SPEAKER_02]: You deal with it by not going back to sensuality.

24
00:03:07,626 --> 00:03:13,447
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the self-loathing disappears, confinement remains, as a factual thing, that it's not unpleasant anymore.

25
00:03:15,352 --> 00:03:18,613
[SPEAKER_02]: And when the confinement remains, no ātavāda can exist outside of it.

26
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:31,496
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you go through the withdrawals and endure the self-loathing, without breaking the precepts, without falling back onto the sensuality, without disregarding the training, you will be destroying the first three fetters.

27
00:03:32,876 --> 00:03:37,457
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, the risk, you might go mad, if you lose your restraint.

28
00:03:38,513 --> 00:03:45,558
[SPEAKER_02]: If you, eh... if you get overwhelmed and act out, you won't get mad if your mind is just spinning out of control.

29
00:03:46,058 --> 00:03:47,519
[SPEAKER_02]: You get mad by acting madly.

30
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:53,484
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you lose sense restraint and if you don't guard your sense doors, that's how you will then lose control.

31
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:54,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Not otherwise.

32
00:03:55,905 --> 00:04:00,308
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, if you don't do anything on account of it, it will have to come down.

33
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:02,850
[SPEAKER_02]: How long that takes?

34
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:05,472
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, depends on how addicted you were before.

35
00:04:06,898 --> 00:04:11,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But I reckon, it would take a while, because we were all quite addicted before.

36
00:04:12,604 --> 00:04:15,807
[SPEAKER_02]: If we weren't, we would have been born in the Buddha's time and become Arahant by now.

37
00:04:25,275 --> 00:04:27,776
[SPEAKER_04]: You start, yeah, virtue, sense of strength.

38
00:04:28,297 --> 00:04:31,558
[SPEAKER_04]: Eventually, you start feeling the confinement that you are in.

39
00:04:31,838 --> 00:04:35,260
[SPEAKER_04]: You're feeling confined by the aggregates, crushed.

40
00:04:36,301 --> 00:04:37,902
[SPEAKER_02]: Which becomes very uninspiring.

41
00:04:38,382 --> 00:04:38,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

42
00:04:39,522 --> 00:04:40,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Where's the bliss?

43
00:04:40,303 --> 00:04:41,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing's worth doing.

44
00:04:41,243 --> 00:04:42,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

45
00:04:42,504 --> 00:04:43,845
[SPEAKER_04]: Just pressure, pressure, pressure.

46
00:04:43,865 --> 00:04:44,645
[SPEAKER_04]: You're just enclosed.

47
00:04:44,925 --> 00:04:45,826
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

48
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:47,006
[SPEAKER_04]: Here I am, enclosed.

49
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:50,991
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do now?

50
00:04:51,031 --> 00:04:51,611
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, there's pain.

51
00:04:51,691 --> 00:04:52,372
[SPEAKER_04]: There's just pain.

52
00:04:52,812 --> 00:05:00,955
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you just keep following that same principle of... that you've been... that God... You want to get rid of the pain.

53
00:05:01,396 --> 00:05:01,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

54
00:05:03,717 --> 00:05:05,718
[SPEAKER_04]: Not trying to get rid of that pain.

55
00:05:05,858 --> 00:05:06,418
[SPEAKER_04]: Enduring it.

56
00:05:07,598 --> 00:05:07,779
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

57
00:05:07,879 --> 00:05:12,541
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, you can be enduring it for the sake of getting it... getting rid of it.

58
00:05:13,421 --> 00:05:14,942
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll endure this when it will go away.

59
00:05:15,142 --> 00:05:16,502
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll endure it so it goes away.

60
00:05:16,623 --> 00:05:16,843
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

61
00:05:18,246 --> 00:05:18,746
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to understand...

62
00:05:18,766 --> 00:05:19,666
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do then?

63
00:05:21,507 --> 00:05:28,708
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm confined now, on account of sense-restraint, nothings we're doing, self-loading, what do I do next?

64
00:05:31,428 --> 00:05:31,928
[SPEAKER_04]: What do I do?

65
00:05:33,369 --> 00:05:37,949
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, discerning the nature of that, again, is... Yeah, okay, yeah, something else, what is that?

66
00:05:37,969 --> 00:05:38,769
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do then?

67
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:42,150
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there anything for me to do?

68
00:05:43,630 --> 00:05:44,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you need to do something?

69
00:05:46,911 --> 00:05:47,691
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's painful.

70
00:05:48,995 --> 00:05:50,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Confinement is unpleasant.

71
00:05:51,618 --> 00:05:52,839
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do?

72
00:05:54,581 --> 00:05:56,443
[SPEAKER_02]: It's painful because you want to do something about it.

73
00:05:57,403 --> 00:05:59,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, include that question in the pain.

74
00:06:00,687 --> 00:06:01,087
[SPEAKER_02]: And I did.

75
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:02,429
[SPEAKER_02]: And the pain is still here.

76
00:06:02,849 --> 00:06:03,369
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do?

77
00:06:04,471 --> 00:06:08,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, keep including it.

78
00:06:08,094 --> 00:06:11,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep including the wish to step out and do something about it.

79
00:06:13,915 --> 00:06:16,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Other than guarding the context, as we said many times.

80
00:06:16,357 --> 00:06:16,837
[SPEAKER_02]: And then what?

81
00:06:17,218 --> 00:06:19,400
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I include the wish to step outside of it?

82
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:19,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

83
00:06:21,261 --> 00:06:24,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Now I can just relax and do stuff?

84
00:06:24,524 --> 00:06:25,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, just... Because I included the wish?

85
00:06:26,085 --> 00:06:26,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, just keep going.

86
00:06:26,705 --> 00:06:29,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do I still not do certain things and do certain things?

87
00:06:31,750 --> 00:06:36,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, sure, there are, like, things to be endured, things to be developed, other things to be developed.

88
00:06:36,033 --> 00:06:41,878
[SPEAKER_02]: But, no, in this particular example, I'm confined, you're all giving me advice of what to do,

89
00:06:42,888 --> 00:06:46,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you just don't do... What is it that I don't do?

90
00:06:46,330 --> 00:06:48,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't break any precepts.

91
00:06:48,812 --> 00:06:50,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't act out of...

92
00:06:50,853 --> 00:06:51,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

93
00:06:51,294 --> 00:06:52,374
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to do anything.

94
00:06:52,394 --> 00:06:52,975
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

95
00:06:53,035 --> 00:06:54,796
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as... What do I do next?

96
00:06:54,876 --> 00:06:55,697
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I do next?

97
00:06:55,757 --> 00:06:59,139
[SPEAKER_02]: Confinement will be confining even more, because you're trying to outrun it.

98
00:07:00,059 --> 00:07:06,023
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you do factually need to stop doing certain things, stop resisting that, so you can calm down, but not too far.

99
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:09,071
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the right balance.

100
00:07:09,111 --> 00:07:10,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Too tight and too loose.

101
00:07:11,211 --> 00:07:13,452
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if it's too much, I just can't bear it.

102
00:07:13,512 --> 00:07:15,293
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't even think about the dhamma anymore.

103
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:17,974
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just so unpleasant, so painful, so self-loathing.

104
00:07:18,034 --> 00:07:18,654
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I do?

105
00:07:19,194 --> 00:07:19,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing.

106
00:07:19,954 --> 00:07:23,336
[SPEAKER_02]: I just should not go back to sensuality and distraction.

107
00:07:23,556 --> 00:07:24,356
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything else fine.

108
00:07:25,356 --> 00:07:27,177
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll sit, I'll walk, I'll sleep.

109
00:07:28,538 --> 00:07:39,767
[SPEAKER_02]: You have something to say, I don't have to practice, I won't practice because it's too painful, but I won't go to sensuality, I won't lose the sense restraint, and I won't act out of desire, greed and distraction.

110
00:07:39,827 --> 00:07:41,169
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do I do?

111
00:07:41,429 --> 00:07:41,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing.

112
00:07:42,029 --> 00:07:46,273
[SPEAKER_02]: Just don't do... literally, don't do anything, which includes don't go back to sensuality.

113
00:07:46,373 --> 00:07:52,117
[SPEAKER_04]: Isn't it like you're still doing that discernment of this is unwholesome?

114
00:07:52,298 --> 00:07:53,378
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you won't be doing it.

115
00:07:54,548 --> 00:07:59,531
[SPEAKER_02]: You can be doing it, but the time will come where even that cannot be sustained.

116
00:07:59,631 --> 00:08:00,871
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to give it up.

117
00:08:02,052 --> 00:08:02,812
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just too painful.

118
00:08:03,713 --> 00:08:09,776
[SPEAKER_02]: And what I'm saying is that will have to come, and when you give it up, give it up, but not all the way.

119
00:08:10,956 --> 00:08:13,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you have not been doing certain things.

120
00:08:14,458 --> 00:08:19,581
[SPEAKER_02]: You have been not, you know, breaking the precepts, you have not been chasing sensuality.

121
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:23,763
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now when you give up the practice as well,

122
00:08:25,997 --> 00:08:31,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Chasing sensuality and breaking the precepts would be doing, would be requiring effort.

123
00:08:32,578 --> 00:08:32,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

124
00:08:33,739 --> 00:08:37,842
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, that would also fall under the principle, no, but I won't do anything.

125
00:08:38,402 --> 00:08:39,123
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't practice.

126
00:08:39,623 --> 00:08:45,167
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't do anything that exerts the effort, because just the thought of effort, it is just too painful.

127
00:08:45,968 --> 00:08:46,728
[SPEAKER_02]: So, okay, fine.

128
00:08:46,908 --> 00:08:48,349
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to make any effort.

129
00:08:48,529 --> 00:08:56,175
[SPEAKER_02]: When that point comes, just drop all the effort, including effort towards sensuality and breaking the precepts that you have been keeping beforehand.

130
00:08:56,455 --> 00:09:02,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because, as you just said, like, you know, chasing sensuality requires effort and maintenance.

131
00:09:02,859 --> 00:09:03,039
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

132
00:09:03,099 --> 00:09:03,939
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a doing.

133
00:09:04,459 --> 00:09:04,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

134
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:13,885
[SPEAKER_02]: See, if somebody, before they have been sufficiently withdrawn, decides to drop the effort because it's too painful, well, sensuality is effortless for them.

135
00:09:14,685 --> 00:09:16,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they haven't been withdrawn from it.

136
00:09:16,106 --> 00:09:16,707
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it, yes.

137
00:09:17,067 --> 00:09:21,430
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, when they drop the effort, they'll do the effortless stuff, which is pursue the sense desires.

138
00:09:21,731 --> 00:09:21,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

139
00:09:22,291 --> 00:09:28,156
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the behavior, as we would say, needs to be developed beforehand, the purity of the body behavior.

140
00:09:28,776 --> 00:09:37,523
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when that has been sufficiently endured, and you reach the wall in terms of, I can't even think about a dhamma anymore, it's just unbearable, fine.

141
00:09:38,384 --> 00:09:43,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, just remember things that you have not been doing, and that's it.

142
00:09:44,928 --> 00:09:45,948
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't have to do anything new.

143
00:09:46,628 --> 00:09:49,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Just stay from... abstaining from certain things.

144
00:09:49,689 --> 00:09:50,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's effortless.

145
00:09:52,570 --> 00:09:55,030
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, whatever's there, you are discerning it.

146
00:09:55,831 --> 00:10:00,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Just not on account of your own intense terms, out of trying to get rid of the pain and so on.

147
00:10:04,173 --> 00:10:05,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, eh...

148
00:10:08,257 --> 00:10:25,485
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see that even, kind of, giving up, in a sense, that effort, or even the, like, you know, it's so painful, and I need to, kind of, keep bearing in mind this pain, or I keep, like, what if I let it go?

149
00:10:25,525 --> 00:10:28,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it might be pleasant, it might be peaceful, it might be okay.

150
00:10:28,826 --> 00:10:33,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's nearly, in itself, kind of, something to relinquish, I suppose.

151
00:10:33,709 --> 00:10:36,250
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea to, kind of, that it, you know,

152
00:10:36,730 --> 00:10:38,191
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to keep bearing the pain.

153
00:10:38,311 --> 00:10:38,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

154
00:10:39,532 --> 00:10:41,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it might change.

155
00:10:41,454 --> 00:10:42,915
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be painful.

156
00:10:43,475 --> 00:10:44,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

157
00:10:45,416 --> 00:10:47,358
[SPEAKER_00]: That's...

158
00:10:47,378 --> 00:10:48,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It is never in your control anyway.

159
00:10:48,619 --> 00:10:49,739
[SPEAKER_00]: That's never in your control anyway.

160
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:55,183
[SPEAKER_02]: But you see, you were trying to control it, in a sense, even... Yeah, well, that's just how the pains of striving work.

161
00:10:55,444 --> 00:11:01,368
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to determine which means going to result, you're going to kind of overdo the tightening, because it was too loose.

162
00:11:02,665 --> 00:11:08,248
[SPEAKER_02]: When things are too loose, you don't just magically stop right in the right tune.

163
00:11:08,528 --> 00:11:10,029
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're going to be over-tightening it.

164
00:11:10,169 --> 00:11:12,971
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, oh, it's going to snap now, right?

165
00:11:13,531 --> 00:11:14,532
[SPEAKER_02]: So let me loosen it.

166
00:11:14,612 --> 00:11:20,655
[SPEAKER_02]: But now you realize, okay, let me loosen it, but not as loose as it was before.

167
00:11:22,116 --> 00:11:24,538
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you will find the right tune, the right balance.

168
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:31,642
[SPEAKER_02]: But, see, when you are already tight to the point of snapping,

169
00:11:32,287 --> 00:11:34,309
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, what do I do now?

170
00:11:35,230 --> 00:11:41,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever I tell you to do, if it's done on that, on that, on account of that intention, will be another twist for tightening it.

171
00:11:42,797 --> 00:11:44,979
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do you do now is just nothing.

172
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:48,662
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, does that mean I go and break the precepts?

173
00:11:48,682 --> 00:11:50,124
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's not nothing, isn't it?

174
00:11:50,764 --> 00:11:51,805
[SPEAKER_02]: So, do nothing.

175
00:11:53,807 --> 00:11:58,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm sleepy, I'm hungry, I'll walk, I'll be silent.

176
00:12:00,018 --> 00:12:00,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'll go and distract him.

177
00:12:00,978 --> 00:12:01,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh-huh.

178
00:12:01,859 --> 00:12:03,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, that's too loose.

179
00:12:04,779 --> 00:12:06,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I'll sit the whole night and think about dhamma.

180
00:12:06,640 --> 00:12:07,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's too tight.

181
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:09,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do now?

182
00:12:09,341 --> 00:12:09,961
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do now?

183
00:12:10,681 --> 00:12:12,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I need to know what do I do now?

184
00:12:14,222 --> 00:12:14,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Right here, right now.

185
00:12:15,382 --> 00:12:16,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that that unpleasant?

186
00:12:16,863 --> 00:12:21,144
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize then it was only unpleasant because you kept trying to sort of get out of it.

187
00:12:21,744 --> 00:12:23,225
[SPEAKER_02]: But then it's, well, actually, I don't know what to do.

188
00:12:23,305 --> 00:12:24,545
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not that unpleasant.

189
00:12:25,362 --> 00:12:26,182
[SPEAKER_02]: I know what I will not do.

190
00:12:26,682 --> 00:12:27,582
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's all I need to know.

191
00:12:28,543 --> 00:12:30,663
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to know specific steps that I need to do.

192
00:12:31,163 --> 00:12:33,603
[SPEAKER_02]: I just need to not forget what I must not do.

193
00:12:34,384 --> 00:12:35,844
[SPEAKER_02]: And not doing things is effortless.

194
00:12:39,705 --> 00:12:41,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you become an island to yourself.

195
00:12:41,825 --> 00:12:42,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

196
00:12:43,165 --> 00:12:44,665
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you realize, oh, the line.

197
00:12:44,865 --> 00:12:47,846
[SPEAKER_02]: The line is the action, intentionally acting out of things.

198
00:12:47,906 --> 00:12:53,487
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not this thought, that thought, this moment, my moment, sensation, slept, forgot, I'm so overwhelmed.

199
00:12:53,547 --> 00:12:53,907
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

200
00:12:54,956 --> 00:13:00,558
[SPEAKER_02]: Only if I intentionally choose to open the door, let the flood in, that's how it gets in.

201
00:13:01,098 --> 00:13:03,159
[SPEAKER_02]: If I don't do it, doesn't matter what's outside.

202
00:13:03,539 --> 00:13:06,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot flood me.

203
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:10,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Intention rooted in greed, aversion, distraction.

204
00:13:10,201 --> 00:13:10,521
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

205
00:13:16,263 --> 00:13:18,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Abstain from those and don't do anything else.

206
00:13:20,104 --> 00:13:20,904
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll be fine.

